[262.621167, "o", "- **Vulnerability Scanner Output**: Inspection of `/sas/vulns.json` yields an empty JSON array (`[]`), confirming that automated security vulnerability scanning detected no exploitable software CVEs on the host.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.621263, "o", "  - Evidence: `/sas/vulns.json`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.621529, "o", "- **Authentication Logs**: Authentication telemetry in `/disk/var/log/secure` demonstrates a classic password brute-force pattern against user `root` from IP `198.51.100.78` between 00:53:24 and 00:54:52 UTC, concluding with successful password authentication at 00:54:55 UTC.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[262.621599, "o", ">   - Evidence: `/disk/var/log/secure`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.621947, "o", "## Reconciling Misattributed CVEs (BlueKeep / CVE-2019-0708)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.621983, "o", "- **Draft Change Ticket Claim**: A pre-filled entry in draft change ticket `CHG-3182` suggested patching `CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep)`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>   - Evidence: `/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> - **Technical Refutation**: \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.622232, "o", "  1. `CVE-2019-0708` (BlueKeep) is a Remote Code Execution vulnerability specific to Microsoft Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) on Windows operating systems.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[262.622369, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.622487, "o", "  2. `ssh-prod-01` is a **CentOS Linux 7** server running OpenSSH. Evidence: `/disk/etc/os-release`, `/disk/etc/ssh/sshd_config`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.622709, "o", "  3. Threat intelligence advisory `/sas/cti/advisory.txt` maps the attack lifecycle to MITRE ATT&CK technique `T1110` (Brute Force) over SSH (`T1021.004`), confirming that no RDP or web application exploit was utilized.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[262.622772, "o", "\u001b[?2004h>   - Evidence: `/sas/cti/advisory.txt`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ## Root Cause Analysis\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.623039, "o", "Rather than a software vulnerability, the compromise resulted from governance and baseline configuration failures:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.623105, "o", "1. **Permissive SSH Daemon Configuration**: `PermitRootLogin yes` and `PasswordAuthentication yes` allowed remote password logins for `root` over SSH."]
[262.623304, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[262.623316, "o", "\u001b[?2004h>    - Evidence: `/disk/etc/ssh/sshd_config`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[262.623514, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.623545, "o", "2. **Weak Credential Baseline**: The `root` account possessed a weak password susceptible to automated dictionary attacks via tools such as Hydra.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.623691, "o", "   - Evidence: `/disk/var/log/secure`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt`.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.623807, "o", "## Conclusion & Recommendations\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[262.624163, "o", "Since no software CVEs were exploited, remediation efforts must focus on credential rotation, SSH daemon hardening, enforcement of key-based authentication, and implementation of network-level and host-level access controls rather than software patch application.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> EOF\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[262.624763, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@d442ccdcc174:/app# "]
[279.379399, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Baseline Security & Configuration Assessment Report\n\n## Overview\nAn audit of host configuration files on `ssh-prod-01` against CIS CentOS Linux 7 Benchmarks and security baseline standards revealed several critical misconfigurations and policy violations. These weaknesses directly enabled the initial compromise, facilitated privilege escalation, and increased host attack surface.\n\n## Summary of Baseline Findings & Non-Compliance\n\n| Capability | Check ID | Configuration File | Finding Description | Current Risk Level |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `BAS-01` | `CHK-04` | `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` | Permissive SSH daemon configuration permitting direct root password login | CRITICAL |\n| `BAS-02` | `CHK-09` | `/etc/my.cnf` | Unrestricted MySQL network binding (`0.0.0.0`) with remote root privileges | HIGH |\n| `PAS-01` | N/A | `/etc/passwd` / `/var/log/secure` | Weak administrative password policy allowing hydra dictionary attack success | CRITICAL |\n| `SUD-01` | N/A | `/etc/sudoers.d/devops` | Unrestricted NOPASSWD sudo grant assigned to user `devops` | CRITICAL |\n| `LOG-01` | N/A | `/var/log/secure` | Local-only log storage lacking remote syslog forwarding, permitting log tampering | HIGH |\n\n## Detailed Configuration Audit & Remediation Guidance\n\n### 1. SSH Server Configuration (`BAS-01` / `CHK-04`)\n- **Observed Configuration**: \n  - `PermitRootLogin yes`: Allows direct remote logins as root.\n  - `PasswordAuthentication yes`: Permits password-based authentication.\n  - `MaxAuthTries 6`: Allows multiple authentication attempts per session without rate limiting or lockout.\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/ssh/sshd_config`, `/sas/baseline.json` (`CHK-04`), `/sas/alerts.json` (`ALERT-03`).\n- **Impact**: Allowed external attacker to brute force the root password over the Internet and gain interactive root shell access.\n- **Baseline Standard Requirement**: \n  - Set `PermitRootLogin no` or `PermitRootLogin prohibit-password`.\n  - Set `PasswordAuthentication no` and enforce SSH key-based authentication (`pubkey`).\n  - Implement `fail2ban` or `sshguard` to dynamically block IPs after 3 failed attempts.\n\n### 2. MySQL Database Configuration (`BAS-02` / `CHK-09`)\n- **Observed Configuration**: \n  - `bind-address = 0.0.0.0`: Database listens on all network interfaces.\n  - Remote `root` user grant enabled.\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/my.cnf`, `/sas/baseline.json` (`CHK-09`), `/sas/alerts.json` (`ALERT-04`).\n- **Impact**: Exposes database daemon to external network scanning and unauthorized remote root connection attempts.\n- **Baseline Standard Requirement**: \n  - Set `bind-address = 127.0.0.1` (or local socket binding).\n  - Revoke remote administrative grants for `root@'%'` and enforce local-only root access (`root@'localhost'`).\n\n### 3. Sudo Privileged Access Configuration\n- **Observed Configuration**: \n  - File `/etc/sudoers.d/devops` contains: `devops ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL`.\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/sudoers.d/devops`.\n- **Impact**: Allows non-root user `devops` to execute any command as `root` without authentication, providing a backdoor foothold.\n- **Baseline Standard Requirement**: \n  - Remove `/etc/sudoers.d/devops` backdoor file.\n  - Restrict sudo grants to specific necessary commands with mandatory password re-authentication (`NOPASSWD` prohibited for general administrative access).\n\n### 4. Administrative Credential Policy\n- **Observed Configuration**: \n  - Root account password was vulnerable to online brute-forcing in under two minutes (~28 attempts).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/log/secure` (`Aug 15 00:53:24` to `00:54:55`).\n- **Impact**: Weak credentials enabled rapid compromise upon discovering open SSH port.\n- **Baseline Standard Requirement**: \n  - Enforce minimum 16+ character complex passphrases for administrative accounts.\n  - Enforce periodic password rotation and disable interactive root password logins entirely.\n\n### 5. Centralized Audit Logging\n- **Observed Configuration**: \n  - System logs (`/var/log/secure`) stored exclusively on local disk without centralized streaming.\n- **Evidence**: `"]
[279.379438, "i", "/disk/var/log/secure` (`--- 15 authentication lines cleared here (T1070.002 log tampering) ---`).\n- **Impact**: Enabled attacker to clear 15 authentication log lines to cover tracks.\n- **Baseline Standard Requirement**: \n  - Configure `rsyslog` or `systemd-journald` to forward security events in real-time to a remote SIEM / append-only log aggregator.\nEOF\n"]
[279.379871, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
